            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>1. Identify balance issues that can interrupt delivery</h2>
<p>An insufficient advertising balance does not always appear as an account suspension. On platforms such as Meta, Google, and TikTok, common symptoms include campaigns stopping when budgets are depleted, declined payment methods, delayed recharge credits, balances consumed by other operators in shared accounts, and restricted learning caused by low balances.</p>
<p>Balance alerts should therefore track more than a fixed balance threshold. Operators need to monitor <strong>available balance, pending charges, recent spend velocity, payment status, and account balance level</strong>.</p>
<p>Start by creating an account inventory in the platform dashboard or YiYu Digital Media. Record the platform, currency, payment method, average daily spend, current balance, balance validity period, and responsible owner. Shared accounts should clearly identify primary operators to prevent duplicate recharges and accidental changes.</p>
<h2>2. Step one: Calculate a safe balance and recharge trigger</h2>
<p>Do not rely on intuition. Use the previous seven days of normal delivery to estimate:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Daily spend = Total spend over the last 7 days ÷ 7<br />
Days of coverage = Current available balance ÷ Daily spend</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Adjust the safety margin for weekends, holidays, product launches, and planned promotions. Account for payment processing delays, shared balance consumption, and budgets that may not update immediately. For shared accounts, calculate the buffer at the account level rather than campaign level.</p>
<p>A practical alert framework has three levels:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Alert level</th>
<th>Reference condition</th>
<th>Required action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Yellow</td>
<td>Less than 3 days of projected coverage</td>
<td>Verify the payment method and upcoming charges</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Orange</td>
<td>Less than 2 days of coverage</td>
<td>Recharge, adjust budgets, and notify the owner</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Red</td>
<td>Insufficient balance or failed payment</td>
<td>Pause expansion and restore the account first</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>These conditions are operating references, not universal thresholds. Review them monthly against actual spend patterns.</p>
<h2>3. Step two: Follow a monitor-confirm-resolve-verify workflow</h2>
<h3>Monitor</h3>
<p>Enable balance, spend-velocity, and payment-status alerts in a unified view. Check them at least once daily and add intraday checks around promotions, livestreams, or major budget changes.</p>
<h3>Confirm</h3>
<p>When an alert arrives, do not immediately recharge again. First determine whether it was caused by accelerated spend, currency changes, failed charges, or another operator using a shared account. Compare the platform balance with internal recharge records.</p>
<h3>Resolve</h3>
<ul>
<li>For a short-term shortfall, add funds while retaining the existing budget;</li>
<li>For a sudden increase in spend, verify whether budgets, creatives, landing pages, or audiences changed before adding funds;</li>
<li>For a failed payment, replace or rebind a valid payment method and check whether the account is restricted;</li>
<li>When multiple accounts trigger alerts, protect campaigns with verified performance and the highest business priority first.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Verify</h3>
<p>After recharging, confirm that the credit is available and that payment charges are working correctly. For campaigns interrupted by a balance issue, review budgets, schedules, audiences, and learning status. Avoid restoring every campaign at once, as simultaneous restarts can create cost volatility.</p>
<h2>4. Step three: Combine auto-recharge with account segmentation</h2>
<p>Stable accounts can use automatic recharging with sensible limits for the amount, frequency, and cumulative recharge cap. Automation solves missing funds, but it does not detect abnormal spend increases, so manual alerts remain necessary.</p>
<p>Segment accounts into core, testing, and backup groups. Core accounts maintain the largest safety buffer. Testing accounts use stricter delivery limits. Backup accounts can receive budget shifts, but operators should not use budget transfers to conceal sustained losses.</p>
<p>A tool-based app once found that its spend had increased sharply over three days even though the balance still looked acceptable. The team discovered that several campaigns had raised their budgets simultaneously, accelerating balance consumption. It introduced account-level spend-velocity alerts and added a separate buffer before the launch period.</p>
<h2>5. Final verification checklist</h2>
<p>After every recharge or budget adjustment, confirm that:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Funds have been credited as available balance;</li>
<li>[ ] The payment method is valid and has no failed charges;</li>
<li>[ ] Priority campaigns did not stop because of depleted budgets;</li>
<li>[ ] Unexpected spend changes have been explained;</li>
<li>[ ] Alert thresholds, owners, and notification channels are current;</li>
<li>[ ] Cost, conversion, and attribution data have been checked after delivery resumes.</li>
</ul>
<p>The objective of balance alerts is not to keep every account permanently funded. It is to identify who should act, what should be changed, and how quickly delivery can be restored. A standardized account inventory, tiered thresholds, and verification checklist turn balance management from emergency troubleshooting into a reliable advertising operation.</p>
